# Generated corpus artefacts. Every one of these is written by a script in `tools/corpus/` — the
# shape is the generator's business, and reformatting them here only guarantees the next
# regeneration undoes it. `policy-corpus.json` is the one that actually matters: `build-bundle.ts`
# emits it minified on purpose because it ships inside the extension, so pretty-printing it would
# inflate the CWS upload for no reader's benefit.
#
# `policy-browse.json` is the same artefact one file over and has to be named here too, or the two
# tools fight: the generator minifies, `pnpm format` pretty-prints, and `format:check` then passes
# until the next regeneration flips it red — a required context going red for a reason nobody's
# change caused. It is the smaller file, so the size argument is weaker and the churn argument is
# identical.
#
# They surfaced when the prettier check was switched from `pull_request_target` to `pull_request`
# and began reading the repository for the first time (#25).
corpus/manifest.json
corpus/curated.json
corpus/analysis-index.json
chrome-extension/public/policy-corpus.json
chrome-extension/public/policy-browse.json

# `tsc` output, emitted from `manifest.ts` by the `ready` script (`pre-build.tsconfig.json` sets
# `outDir: "./"`), and gitignored. Prettier does not read `.gitignore`, so it has to be named here
# or anyone who has run a build gets a red `format:check` locally against a file CI never sees —
# the local/CI divergence this check exists to remove. Formatting it would also be undone by the
# next `pnpm ready` regardless.
chrome-extension/manifest.js

# Site-policy normalizer fixtures. These are read byte-for-byte by `site-policy.test.ts` and
# `policy-diff.test.ts`, and the `stable/` pairs exist to assert that *cosmetic* markup differences
# hash identically — the filenames are the test plan: `whitespace-minified`, `attribute-noise`,
# `entity-encoding`, `wrapper-divs`, `nav-markup`.
#
# Formatting them normalises away precisely the differences under test. The pairs would not go red;
# they would become trivially identical and the suite would keep passing while asserting nothing,
# which is worse. Same reasoning as the generated corpus above: the shape is the fixture's business.
#
# Invisible until now because the CI check's `*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,json}` pattern was root-anchored and
# never read these directories (#69).
packages/unshafted-core/test/fixtures/

# pnpm's lockfile. Generated, enormous, and rewritten wholesale by every install — formatting it
# would be undone by the next `pnpm install` and would turn an already-unreadable diff into a
# worse one. It only ever appeared in prettier's sights because nothing had recursively checked
# the repository root until #69; it was never a deliberate inclusion (#75).
pnpm-lock.yaml
